    title: "Fraternal Love Between Employer and Workman",
    scripture: {
      reference: "James 5:4 (DR)",
      text: "Behold the hire of the labourers, who have reaped down your fields, which by fraud hath been kept back by you, crieth: and the cry of them hath entered into the ears of the Lord of sabaoth.",
    },
    excerpt: {
      workId: "leo-rerum-novarum",
      author: "Pope Leo XIII",
      citation: "Rerum Novarum (1891), §§20–21",
      text: "The great mistake made in regard to the matter now under consideration is to take up with the notion that class is naturally hostile to class, and that the wealthy and the working men are intended by nature to live in mutual conflict. So irrational and so false is this view that the direct contrary is the truth. Just as the symmetry of the human frame is the result of the suitable arrangement of the different parts of the body, so in a State is it ordained by nature that these two classes should dwell in harmony and agreement, so as to maintain the balance of the body politic. Each needs the other: capital cannot do without labour, nor labour without capital. Mutual agreement results in the beauty of good order, while perpetual conflict necessarily produces confusion and savage barbarity.",
    },
    meditation:
      "The arc opens on a sentence Leo XIII published in 1891, the year railroads still ran on coal and the question of whether the workman owed his employer anything besides labor was being fought in the streets of Chicago and Paris. The sentence refuses the most fashionable claim of the century, which was that the classes were natural enemies. Leo will not have it. He says the body politic is one body, that its members need each other in the way a hand needs a wrist, and that the perpetual conflict the agitators were selling was not the truth of the order but its sickness.\n\nThis is the seed of what later doctrine will call solidarity. The word itself, in its modern doctrinal form, belongs to a later pope and is not yours to quote in a project that lives in the public domain. The thing itself — the conviction that I am bound to my neighbor because we belong to the same body — is older than that pope and older than the encyclical. Leo did not invent it. He named it again for an industrial century that had forgotten it. You inherit it from him on this Thursday morning in June.\n\nAttend to where he locates the principle. He does not locate it in sentiment. He does not say employers should feel warmly about workmen, or vice versa. He says they need each other. Capital cannot do without labor, nor labor without capital. The basis of solidarity in the encyclical is structural before it is affective. You are bound to your neighbor first because of what you are — members of a single body — and only second because of how you feel about each other. The feeling is supposed to follow the fact. When the feeling refuses to follow the fact, the body politic is in danger.\n\nThe lawyer reads this with a particular flinch, because the lawyer's daily bread comes from the conflict the encyclical is trying to dissolve. Your client and the opposing party are, in the encyclical's frame, members of the same body, and you are paid to make one member prevail over another. The plaintiffs' bar would rebel at being told they are servants of the body politic when they are obviously servants of one party in it. The defense bar would say the same. But Leo is not pretending the conflicts are not real. He is asking what frame we hold them in. There is a way of representing one member of the body that respects the body. There is a way that does not. The fee is the same in both cases.\n\nThink about the wage-and-hour case you handled last year, the one where the employer had taken the position that the workers were independent contractors so that the overtime hours did not have to be paid. The case settled — most of them settle — and the money was real, and the workers received something. But what was missing through the negotiations was the language Leo uses. No one in the room said, these men are members of your body and you have kept back their hire by fraud, and the cry has reached the ears of the Lord of hosts. Everyone said dollar figures. The dollar figures got the workers some justice, which is not nothing. But the lawyer who reads Rerum Novarum cannot help noticing that an entire vocabulary was missing — the vocabulary that would have let the employer hear what he had actually done, not as a regulatory violation but as a wound to a body he is part of.\n\nThis is the gift solidarity gives the lawyer who can hear it. It gives a language for what the regulatory frame cannot say. The regulatory frame says: you owe them this overtime under section seven of the FLSA. Solidarity says: you owe them more than that, because they are part of the same body you are, and the body is sick when one member is cheated by another. You will not write this in the demand letter. The demand letter has its own grammar and the grammar is useful and the case has to be tried. But you, in your own conscience, can hold the demand letter inside the older frame. The older frame is what keeps you from becoming a fee extractor.\n\nNotice too that Leo's principle cuts both ways. It is not a charter for the workers against the employers. He spends a long passage on the duties of employers to workmen and an equally long passage on the duties of workmen to employers. He insists that the workman is bound never to injure the capital, nor to outrage the person of an employer, never to employ deceit, never to have anything to do with men of evil principles who work upon the laborer with artful promises and excite foolish hopes. He believed in the conflict of duties between the classes, not in the conflict of the classes themselves. The lawyer who learns from him reads opposing parties the same way. Each side has duties. Each side has dignities. The case is a contest about whether the duties have been honored, not a war between two species.\n\nThe letter of James, which is the scripture for today, sharpens this in a way Leo would have recognized. James does not write that the rich are the enemy of the workman. He writes that the hire kept back by fraud cries to the Lord. The structure is preserved: rich and poor are one congregation, one body, addressed by one God. But the fraud is named. The cry is heard. The Lord of hosts — the Lord of armies, in the Hebrew — is not neutral about the workman whose wages are stolen. He is on the workman's side, not because he hates the rich, but because the body is wounded and the wounded part cries out.\n\nThis is where the week begins. Not in slogans. Not in the modern language of class struggle. In something more ancient and harder: that you and your client and your client's adversary are members of a single body, and that the office of advocate exists within that body, not above it. Tomorrow the work moves outward — to the unrepresented, to the pro bono case, to the colleague whose career is collapsing. Today the work moves inward. Read Leo's sentence again. Ask whether you have lived in your practice as if the opposing party were a member of the same body as you. Where you have not, repent. Where you have, give thanks. Then open the file.",
