    weekArcLatin: "Solidaritas",
    title: "When the Bar Devours Its Own",
    scripture: {
      reference: "Galatians 6:1-2 (DR)",
      text: "Brethren, and if a man be overtaken in any fault, you, who are spiritual, instruct such a one in the spirit of meekness, considering thyself, lest thou also be tempted. Bear ye one another's burdens; and so you shall fulfil the law of Christ.",
    },
    excerpt: {
      workId: "augustine-city",
      author: "St. Augustine of Hippo",
      citation: "City of God, Book XIX, ch. 8",
      text: "In the city of this world there are two great evils — error and suffering. Friendship, even when not betrayed, is not without its anxieties; for the friend may suffer, may be in want, may be unjustly accused, and we suffer with him in his suffering. Yet it is better to bear these anxieties than to be free of them by ceasing to love; for to cease to love is to cease to be a man. The friendships even of the wise are full of disquiet, because they look upon their friends as bound to the same lot with themselves, and rejoice in their joys, and grieve in their griefs.",
    },
    meditation:
      "The colleague's name appears in the email from the disciplinary board. You barely know her. You met her once at a CLE three years ago; she gave a panel on appellate practice and you remembered the panel was good. Now her license is on hold pending a hearing, and the rumor in the bar is that the trust account problem is real, that the partner who reported her was a partner she had refused to sleep with, that the firm is closing ranks, that the bar counsel is moving quickly to look serious in front of the press. You do not know which parts of the rumor are true. You do not know her side. You delete the email and refill your coffee.\n\nThis is one of the harder tests of solidarity, and the bar does not pass it often. The instinct in the profession, when a colleague comes under investigation, is to step back. The reasons are reasonable. You do not know the facts. You have your own license to protect. The accused may be guilty. Defending her publicly may align you with conduct you would not endorse. The whisper network's interpretation is probably wrong, but you cannot prove it without information you do not have. Better to wait. Better to keep your head down. Better to let the process work.\n\nMost of the time, this is what happens. The accused lawyer is left alone with the process. Her firm distances itself within a week. Her clients receive the standard letter and find other counsel. The few friends who reach out do so privately and ask her not to share that they did. The bar association issues no statement. Her name disappears from the section listservs. By the time the hearing arrives, she has been alone for months. The hearing may go in her favor or against her, but either way, what she will remember for the rest of her life is the loneliness of the months before it.\n\nThe Letter to the Galatians addresses this directly, and the addressing is exactly what the modern bar will not do. Brethren, if a man be overtaken in any fault. The verse does not begin with proof of guilt. It begins with the structural fact: a man has been overtaken. Something has fallen on him, whether by his fault or another's, whether deserved or not. The verb is overtaken, the Greek prolambano, the way a wave catches you when you were not watching the water. Paul is not interested first in whether the man deserved the wave. He is interested in what the brethren do now.\n\nThe instruction is to restore — the Greek katartizo, to set a bone, to mend a net. The work is technical and patient. It is not exoneration; it does not pretend nothing happened. It is restoration: putting back into place what was knocked out. Notice also the phrase considering thyself, lest thou also be tempted. Paul is realistic about why we step back from the accused colleague. We step back because we are afraid the contagion will reach us. The afraid response, he says, is exactly backwards. The closer you are to the one who has fallen, the more clearly you see the structures that could fell you next.\n\nAugustine, in the City of God passage, understands what makes this hard. Friendship is not without anxieties. The friend may be in want, may be unjustly accused, and we suffer with him. The temptation to free oneself from those anxieties by ceasing to love is constantly present. He calls it the temptation to cease being human. The friendships of the wise, he says, are full of disquiet because the wise refuse the exit. They take their friends as bound to the same lot with themselves. The lot of the accused colleague is your lot too, even if you have done nothing wrong.\n\nThis does not commit you to defending conduct you believe was wrong. The Galatians passage assumes the fault is real. The instruction is not to deny it. The instruction is to bear one another's burdens during the process of dealing with it. To bear a burden is not to deny that the burden exists. It is to put your shoulder under the same load. With the colleague under investigation, this looks like specific things, none of them spectacular. A phone call: I heard, I am sorry, can I take you to lunch this week. A presence in the gallery on the hearing date, so that her face when she walks in is not the first face she sees that day. A letter to the bar counsel describing what you know of her character from your own dealings, accurate to your own knowledge, no more, no less. A referral, after the hearing whatever its outcome, to a recovery program or counselor you trust.\n\nThe bar's culture punishes this kind of conduct. To stand visibly beside a colleague under investigation is to invite the question of why. It is to associate yourself with conduct that may turn out to be discrediting. It is to ask your name to be spoken in the same sentence as hers in the corridor whispers. The Christian lawyer does not pretend these costs are imaginary. He weighs them. And he concludes, with Augustine, that the alternative — to cease loving in order to be safe — is to cease being human, and that a human cost is the price he is willing to pay to remain one.\n\nThere is also a question of whether the discipline itself is just. The bar's processes are not infallible. They are run by humans. They sometimes act on bad evidence. They are sometimes pressured by interests outside the matter. They sometimes select certain offenders for vigorous prosecution while overlooking others. The colleague under investigation may be guilty of what she is charged with and may also be the victim of a process that is hunting her for reasons beside the charges. Solidarity does not require you to believe she is innocent. It requires you to refuse to let the process operate on her in your silence. Where the process is fair, your support does not impede it. Where the process is unfair, your support is what may bend it back toward fairness.\n\nNotice the situation Paul addresses next. Bear ye one another's burdens. The verb is present continuous. Not bear one another's burden, singular, once. Bear them, plurally, in the ongoing way that a household does. The fault you carry today, your colleague will carry next year for you. The accused this season is the supporter next season. The bar that has cultivated this practice — and there are bars that have, mostly the smaller ones, mostly the older ones — has cultivated the law of Christ inside the law of the state.\n\nThe accused colleague's hearing is in two weeks. You barely know her. You can still write the letter today. The letter does not have to be long. It has to be true and it has to bear her name in your hand without flinching. That is what solidarity actually looks like at this point in your week — not a slogan about workers in 1891, but a stamped envelope on your desk on Sunday night.",
